# ${{ values.name }}

${{ values.description }}

This agent uses the Phase 1 LangGraph draft → human review → revise/approve
workflow, PostgreSQL checkpoints, Aiven AI Gateway and self-hosted Langfuse.
It is scaffolded and has **not been deployed**. The template opens a draft pull
request in the same repository and does not create infrastructure or credentials.

## Develop locally

From `agents/${{ values.name }}` after the pull request is merged:

```sh
cp environment.example .env
# Generate a DIFFERENT value for each of the four blank local secrets:
openssl rand -hex 24
# Set POSTGRES_PASSWORD, APP_PASSWORD, SESSION_SECRET and APP_API_KEY in .env.
docker compose up --build
```

Open <http://localhost:8080>. Local Compose creates its own PostgreSQL container
and volume, uses mock model responses and disables tracing until configured.
The Dockerfile expects the **repository root** as its context; Compose already
sets that context. Use a different host port when running several agents locally.

Edit `workflow.py` to change the graph. Preserve the checkpoint thread ID and
avoid side effects before the review `interrupt`, because that node runs again
when resumed. Run the inherited checks from this folder:

```sh
python3 -m venv .venv
.venv/bin/pip install -r requirements-dev.txt -c constraints.txt
.venv/bin/python -m pytest
```

The PostgreSQL integration checks require `TEST_DATABASE_URL` pointing at a
dedicated test database, or `TEST_LOCAL_POSTGRES=1` with `pgserver==0.1.4`
installed. They create and remove only their own randomly named schema.

## Prepare existing services

1. In Aiven project `${{ values.aivenProject }}`, reuse PostgreSQL service
   `${{ values.postgresService }}` and create a separate database named
   `${{ values.name | replace('-', '_') }}`. Prefer a dedicated database user with
   access only to that database. Use its full PostgreSQL connection URI as
   `DATABASE_URL`. **Do not reuse `defaultdb` or another agent's database.** This
   starter uses fixed table names and sharing a database exposes other agents'
   runs. No migration or deletion of existing databases is needed.
2. Download the project's PostgreSQL CA certificate and base64 encode its PEM
   contents as `PG_CA_CERT_BASE64`. Production enforces hostname verification
   (`sslmode=verify-full`); never set `LOCAL_DEVELOPMENT=true` on Runtime.
3. In [the existing Langfuse instance](${{ values.langfuseUrl }}), create or choose
   the intended project and use its **Settings → API Keys** to create project
   credentials. Confirm its ID is `${{ values.langfuseProjectId }}`; if it differs,
   update `runtime.json` and `catalog-info.yaml`. Add `LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY` and
   `LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY` as Runtime secrets. These are project keys, not instance
   administration keys. No Langfuse redeploy or new storage is required.
4. To call Aiven AI Gateway, set `MODEL_MODE=openai-compatible` and add
   `MODEL_API_KEY` as a Runtime secret. Confirm `${{ values.modelName }}` is
   available to that Gateway key. `mock` mode makes no model calls.

This minimal template documents project/key creation in Langfuse's UI. It does
not require organization-administration credentials in Backstage. When using a
shared Langfuse project deliberately, runs are distinguishable by session UUID
and `APP_ENVIRONMENT`; access to that project exposes all of its agent traces.

Before deployment, verify the key pair belongs to the intended project using
the repository helper. With `LANGFUSE_BASE_URL`, `LANGFUSE_PROJECT_ID`,
`LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY` and `LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY` supplied privately in your shell
environment, run this from the agent directory:

```sh
python3 ../../agent-hub/scripts/langfuse_setup.py
```

The helper makes a read-only project API call, checks the project ID and prints
no keys. Its optional `--write-env` destination must be outside Git and creates
a private file without overwriting an existing one.

## Deploy on Aiven Runtime

Merge the pull request first. In project `${{ values.aivenProject }}`, create a
**new** Runtime service named `${{ values.name }}` in `${{ values.cloud }}` using
plan `${{ values.plan }}` (verify availability for the project). Set:

| Setting | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Repository | `https://github.com/carabaestlein/aiven-prototype-agent.git` |
| Branch | `${{ values.baseBranch }}` |
| Build context | `./` (repository root) |
| Dockerfile | `./agents/${{ values.name }}/Dockerfile` |
| HTTP port | `8080` |
| Health check | `/health/ready` |

`runtime.json` is the Aiven API create-service body for
`POST /v1/project/${{ values.aivenProject }}/service`; `compose.aiven.yaml`
shows the equivalent build and public environment settings. They contain no
credentials. Supply the following secrets before starting the service:

| Runtime secret | Source |
| --- | --- |
| `DATABASE_URL` | URI for this agent's isolated database on `${{ values.postgresService }}` |
| `PG_CA_CERT_BASE64` | Base64 PEM for that PostgreSQL project's CA |
| `APP_PASSWORD` | New independent login secret, at least 16 characters |
| `SESSION_SECRET` | New independent session secret, at least 32 characters |
| `APP_API_KEY` | New independent API secret, at least 32 characters |
| `MODEL_API_KEY` | Aiven AI Gateway key, required in real model mode |
| `LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY` | Public key for Langfuse project `${{ values.langfuseProjectId }}` |
| `LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY` | Matching secret key for that project |

Retain the generated public variables from `runtime.json`. Set `APP_RELEASE` to
the merged commit SHA for trace correlation. Runtime API secret entries use
`{"key": "NAME", "value": "PRIVATE_VALUE", "kind": "secret"}`; construct
any enriched API payload outside Git with private file permissions. Never paste
it into Backstage parameters, task output, a pull request or a committed file.
Prefer the Runtime secret editor if you do not already have deployment tooling.

This template intentionally omits a default `application_service_credential`
integration: the Phase 1 integration yields `defaultdb`, which would share run
tables across agents. Use an integration only if its generated connection
string selects this agent's isolated database. Keys and the database connection
remain independent from the first deployed agent.

After Runtime reports running, take its actual public HTTPS endpoint and check
`/health/ready`, log in, create a synthetic run, revise it and approve it. A
healthy container alone does not verify model calls or trace export. Check the
same run/session UUID in [Langfuse traces](${{ values.langfuseUrl }}/project/${{ values.langfuseProjectId }}/traces).
`TRACE_CONTENT=false` keeps request and draft bodies out of traces; enable it
only intentionally for appropriate data. The graph and HTTP model call are
instrumented explicitly. Langfuse export failures do not retry model execution.

## Register in Agent Hub

After merge, choose **Create → Register Existing Component** and enter:

```text
https://github.com/carabaestlein/aiven-prototype-agent/blob/${{ values.baseBranch }}/agents/${{ values.name }}/catalog-info.yaml
```

The generated catalog entry is `experimental` and marked `scaffolded`. After
the deployment checks pass, add links and the `aiven.io/runtime-url` and
`aiven.io/endpoint` annotations for the actual Runtime console service and
application endpoint; add its readiness link and set `aiven.io/deployment-state` to
`deployed` and choose the appropriate lifecycle. Registering source code does
not imply that a deployment exists. Catalog registration is deferred until
merge so it does not depend on a pull-request branch that can be deleted.

See `PROVENANCE.md` for source, dependency pins and licenses.
